+      <h3 style="color: var(--accent4);">⚡ Honest Assessment of "Key Neural Intelligence Reasoning Validation"</h3>
+      <p>
+        <strong>The acronym is doing too much work.</strong> "Key Neural Intelligence Reasoning
+        Validation" is a seven-syllable descriptor of a technology, not a brand. Buyers do not
+        care what the letters stand for. They care what the product does for them. "KNIRV" as
+        a spoken word is difficult — it starts with a consonant cluster (KN) that English
+        speakers will mispronounce half the time (neerv? k-nirv? k-nirve?). Memorable brands
+        either have clean phonetics or strong category ownership. "KNIRV" has neither yet.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        <strong>This is fixable but requires a decision.</strong> Option A: lean into the
+        technical acronym — own the "KNIRV" brand with strong category ownership in AI
+        governance (like "Kubernetes" became "K8s" — ugly at first, owned through dominance).
+        Option B: find a simpler product name for the commercial product while keeping KNIRV
+        as the protocol/network name. The closed-source KNIRVSERVER could ship under a
+        cleaner commercial name (e.g., "Forge", "Sentinel", "Anchor") while the open-source
+        network retains the KNIRV brand.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        <strong>The value proposition in the acronym is also misleading for two of the five
+        strategies.</strong> "Reasoning Validation" implies LLM output quality checking —
+        which is what Guardrails AI, NeMo Guardrails, and others do. KNIRVSERVER's actual
+        differentiation is <em>execution environment enforcement</em>, not reasoning validation.
+        The acronym points buyers to the wrong competitive set.
+      </p>
